% ============================================================================
% simile-meaning — common English similes and what they actually mean
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A simile compares one thing to another using "like" or "as" — "as brave
% as a lion" doesn't literally describe a lion, it describes courage.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? simile_meaning(as_brave_as_a_lion, $M)
%       % extremely_courageous
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `simile_meaning(simile, meaning)` carrying the source citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the meaning WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls,
% and abstains on any simile not one of these three source-cited examples.
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a meaning and recall which simile
% carries it:
%
%     ? simile_meaning($S, free_or_unrestricted)
%       % as_free_as_a_bird
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why `simile` is an apostrophe/punctuation-free
% underscore-joined atom label rather than the punctuated phrase: ADJ atom
% labels cannot contain spaces or punctuation, so this table uses the same
% underscore-joined atom-style-label discipline `idiom-meaning.adj` already
% established for multi-word phrases — `as_brave_as_a_lion` stands for "as
% brave as a lion." The header prose below quotes the ORIGINAL phrasing, so
% the mapping remains independently checkable against the cited page.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Simile: Definition and
% Examples" article's "Common simile examples" table, which pairs each
% simile with its meaning directly:
%
%     simile (this table's atom label)   meaning                 simile phrase (verbatim)
%     ----------------------------------  ----------------------  ---------------------------
%     as_brave_as_a_lion                  extremely_courageous    "As brave as a lion"
%     like_a_needle_in_a_haystack         very_difficult_to_find  "Like a needle in a haystack"
%     as_free_as_a_bird                   free_or_unrestricted    "As free as a bird"
%
% WebFetch-verified before writing. `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a
% reputable, widely-cited grammar-content company, the same tier this
% stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov language sources and the same
% source family already cited by `sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj`/
% `contraction.adj`/`possessive-noun.adj`. A simile not one of these
% three — e.g. `as_busy_as_a_bee`, a real, well-known simile, but not one
% of this source's own cited table rows — is deliberately NOT a row, so a
% recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a meaning. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table simile_meaning {
    columns simile, meaning

    row (as_brave_as_a_lion, extremely_courageous)
    row (like_a_needle_in_a_haystack, very_difficult_to_find)
    row (as_free_as_a_bird, free_or_unrestricted)

    source "As brave as a lion"
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/rhetorical-devices/simile/"
    trust consensus
}
